// The six alphabets of RFC 4648, as one type — and the collision that made it worth doing.
//
// `packages/codec` is three files, one per base, and two of them declare a constant with the same
// name and the same value for different things:
//
//     base64.wac:15  export i32 ALPHABET_STANDARD() { return 0; }   // A-Za-z0-9+/
//     base32.wac:15  export i32 ALPHABET_STANDARD() { return 0; }   // A-Z2-7
//     base64.wac:17  export i32 ALPHABET_URL()      { return 1; }   // A-Za-z0-9-_
//     base32.wac:17  export i32 ALPHABET_HEX()      { return 1; }   // 0-9A-V
//
// So `base64.encode(data, base32.ALPHABET_HEX(), false)` type-checks, and produces base64url. The two
// closed sets are `i32`, they overlap exactly, and the compiler has nothing to say. A caller who
// imports both files — which `@/packages/url` and `@/packages/http` both would — has four names in
// scope holding two distinct values between them.
//
// That is `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s *closed set spelled as a string or an integer* with the sharpest
// version of the consequence: not a value outside the set, but a value **inside a different set**.

import { Bytes } from "core";

/**
 * An alphabet, and with it the base.
 *
 * The base is not a separate argument because it is not a separate fact: there is no such thing as
 * base32 with a base64 alphabet, and the shipped package has three `encode`s to enforce by
 * separation what one enum enforces by construction.
 */
export enum Alphabet {
  /** §8, lowercase. The only base16 the shipped `hex.wac` calls `encode`. */
  Base16Lower,
  /** §8 proper — RFC 4648's base16 is uppercase, and `hex.wac` spells it `encodeUpper`. */
  Base16Upper,
  /** §6, `A-Z2-7`. */
  Base32,
  /**
   * §6's alphabet in lowercase, which the RFC does not define and a caller needed within the day.
   *
   * `@/packages/tor/src/onionaddr.wac` is the caller: a v3 onion address is
   * `base32(PUBKEY | CHECKSUM | VERSION) + ".onion"` and is written lowercase, so the shipped
   * package encodes with `ALPHABET_STANDARD()` and then **hand-writes an ASCII case fold in both
   * directions**, five lines each way, around the codec it just called.
   *
   * This is the second instance of the thing the README warned about one paragraph after making the
   * decision — *"the type encodes what the package does rather than what the RFC says … worth
   * knowing before the next enum is named after a specification"* — with `Base16Lower` as the first.
   * Two independent callers is not a pattern yet and it is enough to stop calling it an exception.
   */
  Base32Lower,
  /** §7, `0-9A-V` — extended hex, which sorts in the same order as the data. */
  Base32Hex,
  /** §4, `A-Za-z0-9+/`. */
  Base64,
  /** §5, `A-Za-z0-9-_`. */
  Base64Url,

  /** 4, 5 or 6. The whole of what distinguishes the three bases. */
  i32 bits(const this) {
    match (this) {
      Base16Lower: { return 4; }
      Base16Upper: { return 4; }
      Base32:      { return 5; }
      Base32Lower: { return 5; }
      Base32Hex:   { return 5; }
      Base64:      { return 6; }
      Base64Url:   { return 6; }
    }
  }

  /**
   * How many digits a full group is: `lcm(8, bits) / bits` — 2, 8 and 4.
   *
   * **This is where generalising costs something**, and `packages/codec/src/base32.wac` said so first
   * about its own numbers: *"Those numbers are not a pattern anyone remembers, so they are a table
   * rather than arithmetic: `PAD_FOR` and `DIGITS_FOR`, indexed by the number of leftover bytes."*
   *
   * That is a good reason and it is an argument against this method. §6 of the RFC states the
   * padding for each leftover count in a table a reader can check line by line; a formula is right
   * or wrong all at once. See the note at the foot of `./rfc4648.wac` — the two forms disagree about
   * what a reviewer can do, and nothing in this directory has that trade-off written down.
   */
  i32 groupDigits(const this) { … }

  /** The digit for a value in `0 .. 2^bits`. */
  u8 digit(const this, i32 v) { … }

  /**
   * The value of a digit, or nothing.
   *
   * `T?` rather than `-1`, which the shipped `digitValue` uses — and it is exported from all three
   * files with two different arities, `digitValue(i32 c)` in `hex.wac` and `base64.wac` and
   * `digitValue(i32 c, i32 alphabet)` in `base32.wac`. Three exports, one meaning, no relation.
   */
  i32? value(const this, u8 c) { … }
}

/**
 * Whether the encoding is padded to a whole group.
 *
 * **A named pair, because the shipped call site is `encode(data, ALPHABET_URL(), false)`** and the
 * `false` is unreadable at the call. `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has the entry about a closed set spelled
 * as an integer; a `bool` parameter is the two-element case of it, and the README's own example is
 * the evidence — it is the line a reader meets first and the one that says least.
 *
 * Base16 has no short group, so `Padded` and `Unpadded` mean the same thing there. That is a fact
 * about base16 rather than a hole: `groupDigits() == 2` and a byte is two digits.
 */
export enum Padding { Padded, Unpadded }
